{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795290541,"nonce":"McaJkduoTXcIhcbXOoV3Zgf6qR8=","title":"Immix: collect while several threads allocate","message":"Design only. No code is written. Parent: 5c7208a. This blocks making Immix the default heap.\n\nDecision. Immix stops every mutator thread at a call site, collects on one thread, and resumes them. A thread stops inside an allocation call or inside a declared blocking region. There are no signals and no thread suspension. This keeps the rule the whole collector relies on: a collection starts only at a call, where compiled code keeps every live pointer in a stack slot or a callee-saved register.\n\nToday. The first thread that claims the heap owns collection. Any other thread that allocates sets FOREIGN, and collection stops for the process. The owner restriction has three causes. The fiber state and the hidden stack ranges are thread-local, so only a thread can list its own stack windows. Compiled code is safe to scan only at calls. The heap locks assume no thread is stopped while it holds one.\n\nThread records. A thread registers at its first slow-path allocation or at claim_owner. Its record holds the stack top, a state (running, parked, native, dead) and the roots it published. Registration and exit take the world lock. A thread that never allocates from the heap never registers, so compile threads cost nothing. thread_enter only records the stack top for a later registration.\n\nStopping. The collector takes the world lock, sets a global STOP flag and waits until every other record is parked, native or dead. Then it marks and sweeps, bumps the epoch, clears STOP and wakes the others. It holds the world lock for the whole collection, so a thread that registers, exits or leaves a native region waits there. The bump path stays as it is. The slow paths (next_region and alloc_large) read STOP with one relaxed load. A thread that sees it parks.\n\nRoots per thread. The fiber backend can answer only for the calling thread. So a thread that parks first spills its callee-saved registers. Then it computes its own stack windows, with fiber stacks included and hidden ranges removed. It also lists its held addresses. It stores both in its record. The collector scans its own windows and every published one.\n\nNative regions. Blocking work runs inside blocking(|| ...): join, contended mutex lock, channel receive, sleep, park, and the waits of the band pool. Entering publishes roots like a park and marks the thread native. The thread touches no heap object until it leaves. Leaving takes the world lock, so it waits out a collection. The caller must not keep a heap pointer only in the frames below the blocking call. Plugins reach the hooks the way they reach the allocator now: zrtl::heap gets set_blocking(enter, leave) and the host installs it.\n\nOpen regions. SMALL and MEDIUM stay thread-local. A thread does not retire them before it stops. After any wait that spanned a collection (park, leaving a native region, registering) it resets them and charges the bytes used. This is correct because the sweep treats every unmarked line as free, including the unused tail of a region.\n\nChoosing the collector. When since_gc reaches the threshold, any registered thread starts a collection. A thread that finds a stop in progress parks instead of starting a second one, and reads the threshold again after it resumes.\n\nTimeout. The collector waits at most 20 ms. A thread that spins on an atomic, or blocks without a native region, cannot answer. On timeout the collector cancels the stop and wakes everyone. The next attempt waits for another floor of allocation, and the wait doubles on each failure up to 8 floors. A success resets it. The trace names the thread that did not answer. A missed annotation costs memory and one log line, never a hang. atomic_load and yield_now also poll the flag. Explicit collectgarbage and gc.collect wait up to one second.\n\nHidden pointers. This is the larger part of the work. Today a second thread turns collection off. When a heap pointer sits in memory the collector does not read, nothing checks that it stays reachable. Known cases: the closure environment that thread_spawn_closure moves into a Box, channel payloads from send_ptr, values in promise states, thread results. Each needs a Hold or Held storage until the receiving side owns the pointer. Audit zrtl_thread, promise.rs and the band pool before the flag defaults on.\n\nRollout. ZYNTAX_IMMIX_THREADS=1 turns multi-thread collection on. Without it, a second allocating thread still turns collection off. The default changes after the stress runs pass on both machines.\n\nFreed-block reuse (py any_field). This is independent and lands first. free of a small block that ends exactly at the thread's SMALL.cur rolls cur back and clears the start byte. The bump path does not change. It covers allocate, use, free loops, the any_field shape. A free from another thread finds no match and is ignored. If rows still lose after this, per-size free lists come next.\n\nOrder of work:\n1. Rollback free. Measure any_field and the 43 rows.\n2. Thread records, stop and resume, own-roots publication, timeout. Flag off by default.\n3. blocking() and the zrtl hooks. Annotate zrtl_thread, the band pool, promise threads.\n4. Hidden-pointer audit and fixes.\n5. Stress and benchmarks, then the default.\n\nTests:\n- Eight threads, each holding a growing list on its stack, collecting at every refill (ZYNTAX_IMMIX_STRESS). Every list stays intact and garbage is freed.\n- A thread inside blocking() while another allocates. Collections complete with no timeout.\n- A thread that spins with no safepoint. The collection cancels in about 20 ms, the backoff doubles, the program ends.\n- Ten thousand threads that start, allocate and exit during collections.\n- A worker with suspended coroutines that holds the only reference to an object. Another thread starts the collection. The object survives.\n- A ZynML program with spawn and join workers that build trees. Output equals the pool's. Peak memory stays bounded.\n- All of the above looped on the Mac (arm64) and the NUC (x86) for the memory model.\n- The 43 rows stay at a geomean of 1.00. Add a four-thread row set.\n\nNot chosen:\n- Signals or Mach thread suspension. A thread can stop at any instruction, where compiled code does not keep every pointer in a slot. It can also stop while it holds malloc or a runtime lock the collector needs, which gives rare hangs.\n- A heap per thread. Pointers cross threads.\n- A leaking side heap for non-owner threads. Memory grows without bound.\n- Concurrent marking. It needs a write barrier that compiled code does not have.\n\nOut of scope here: parallel marking, returning memory to the OS, line-level evacuation.","files":null}]}